title: '2026-11-14: A reply in the unread list was not lost for the reader, but its message stayed unread; now the reply files the message, committed as c5d10a65.'
pid: 35245c01-235a-4904-bf68-fdbd7427208a
body: |2-
  Your mission, bbdab1fa ("Find out whether a reply Jeff types under a
  message ... is lost at the save"). What was found and built is in my
  note "Writing in an unintegrated child, where the subscribee as such
  draws it, integrates it." under "Integrating is containing; dismissing
  is hiding.", and the third party's case under "Consequence: a fork
  made in such a view vanishes from it."; your question about containing
  a reply is answered in "? Containing an overrider should count as
  integrating the original it stands for. Not built." I read "two owned
  skgrepos" as one owned by each party: in each test the reader owns its
  skgrepo and the sender's is foreign, as on Jeff's server.
  Evidence: tests/fork.rs, five sub-tests on the fixtures
  'tests/fork/fixtures-mail' and '-third-party'; the first failed before
  the change at "the reply must file m1 in f". 'bash/all-tests.sh'
  printed "All tests passed." after the review's fixes. Driven once more
  on a scratch server through skg-for-ai: the reply under "message one"
  saved, the list went from 'C2/' to 'C1/2', and "message one" with the
  reply stood last in the reader's note. Two fresh reviewers read the
  code and the tests; all their findings but one are fixed (listed in my
  note); the one declined is a subscriber drawn write-protected.
contains:
- af65bc6e-dfea-426c-8595-678be6072a1f
- d4fae331-e6b0-4500-8b42-6b86708cf0f6
